    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Could you get a bit more technical on the wear resistance side? Like, how do they compare in terms of surface hardness or resistance to scratches and scuffs from heavy movement? I want to make sure we don't end up with a floor that looks worn down in a year.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Polished concrete tops the chart in terms of surface hardness. With a Mohs hardness rating between 6 and 7, it resists scratches and scuffs exceptionally well, maintaining appearance over time. LVT is also hard and scratch-resistant, generally providing a surface hardness rating of around 60 to 90 on the Mohs scale, which is significantly higher than natural stone. Natural stone varies widely in hardness, but it's typically at least 6 on the Mohs scale, so it resists scratches to a certain extent, although high polish stones like marble can be more susceptible to scuffs. In a high-traffic commercial lobby, the extreme polish of LVT may provide the best compromise between durability and show, with less frequent need for refinishing.",
      "source": "target",
