    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful. One thing though—we've had issues in previous buildings where certain floors just seemed to get marked up or scuffed in weird, irregular patterns that didn't always match the main foot traffic paths. Is that common with these materials, or could it be something else affecting the surface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That phenomenon, where marks or scuffs appear irregular or seemingly out of the 'main' flow of traffic, can occur with any flooring material but is less common with those mentioned for high-traffic areas. It could be due to placed objects like furniture or people, or other ambient factors such as airborne debris. In the case of polished concrete and LVT, it's impossible for the subfloor material to show great differentiation on the surface, making irregular patterns challenging to attribute to the material itself. With natural stone, some variances in firmness and porosity might lead to dissimilar wear patterns, but also, as previously mentioned, individuals can spillage activities on stone can become exaggerated over time. Regular maintenance, education of the personnel using the lobby, and perhaps a slight reduction of moving items when not in use might help prevent these sorts of issues.",
